mon multiple of the first $25$ positive integers is equal to $26A7114B4C0$. Find $100 \\times A + 10 \\times B + C$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e7465922015f399a7547e7c1d07849b854c39af7af6272375cf9401c54a455e0"},"seconds":20.129932165145874,"seed":620600411833885645,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we first need to determine the least common multiple (LCM) of the first 25 positive integers. The prime factorization of the first 25 positive integers is as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&1 = 1, \\\\\n&2 = 2, \\\\\n&3 = 3, \\\\\n&4 = 2^2, \\\\\n&5 = 5, \\\\\n&6 = 2 \\cdot 3, \\\\\n&7 = 7, \\\\\n&8 = 2^3, \\\\\n&9 = 3^2, \\\\\n&10 = 2 \\cdot 5, \\\\\n&11 = 11, \\\\\n&12 = 2^2 \\cdot 3, \\\\\n&13 = 13, \\\\\n&14 = 2 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n&15 = 3 \\cdot 5, \\\\\n&16 = 2^4, \\\\\n&17 = 17, \\\\\n&18 = 2 \\cdot 3^2, \\\\\n&19 = 19, \\\\\n&20 = 2^2 \\cdot 5, \\\\\n&21 = 3 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n&22 = 2 \\cdot 11, \\\\\n&23 = 23, \\\\\n&24 = 2^3 \\cdot 3, \\\\\n&25 = 5^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in these factorizations. Therefore, the LCM of the first 25 positive integers is:\n\n\\[\n2^4 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 17 \\cdot 19 \\cdot 23.\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate this product step by step:\n\n\\[\n2^4 = 16,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3^2 = 9,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n5^2 = 25,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2^4 \\cdot 3^2 = 16 \\cdot 9 = 144,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n144 \\cdot 25 = 3600,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3600 \\cdot 7 = 25200,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n25200 \\cdot 11 = 277200,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n277200 \\cdot 13 = 3603600,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3603600 \\cdot 17 = 61261200,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n61261200 \\cdot 19 = 1163962800,\n\\]\n\n\\[\n1163962800 \\cdot 23 = 26771144400.\n\\]\n\nHowever, we need to match this with the given format \\(26A7114B4C0\\). By comparing \\(26771144400\\) with \\(26A7114B4C0\\), we see that \\(A = 7\\), \\(B = 1\\), and \\(C = 4\\).\n\nFinally, we calculate \\(100 \\times A + 10 \\times B + C\\):\n\n\\[\n100 \\times 7 + 10 \\times 1 + 4 = 700 + 10 + 4 = 714.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{714}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{714}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:304"}
